    {
      "title": "Stability Selection",
      "authors": "Nicolai Meinshausen; Peter Bühlmann",
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/0809.2932v2",
      "pdf_url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/0809.2932v2",
      "version": "arXiv:0809.2932v2, revised 2009-05-16; published work 2010",
      "retrieved_utc": "2026-10-16T05:54:07Z",
      "read_scope": "Abstract/version page; PDF Section2, Definition1, group-set discussion, Theorem1 and assumptions (PDF pp4–8; zero-based3–7).",
      "paraphrase": "Selection frequencies summarize subsampling sensitivity, including sets of variables. Section2 assumes i.i.d. observations. The expected false-selection bound additionally assumes exchangeability of noise selections and a selector no worse than random guessing. This controls an expected count under those premises, not arbitrary dependent-data FDR or ranking utility.",
      "fulltext_archived": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Variable selection with error control: Another look at Stability Selection",
      "authors": "Rajen D. Shah; Richard J. Samworth",
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1105.5578v2",
      "pdf_url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1105.5578v2",
      "version": "arXiv:1105.5578v2, revised 2011-10-05; journal DOI10.1111/j.1467-9868.2011.01034.x",
      "retrieved_utc": "2026-10-16T05:54:07Z",
      "read_scope": "Abstract/version page; PDF Section2 i.i.d. assumption/CPSS definition, Section3.1 Theorem1, shape-restriction discussion; Appendix independence step exposed by targeted find.",
      "paraphrase": "CPSS aggregates complementary half-sample selections. Its general bounds concern low-selection-probability inclusions and high-selection-probability exclusions, without exchangeability or selector-quality conditions. The data framework still assumes i.i.d. units; tighter bounds add shape constraints. Ordinary bootstrap and user/time dependence do not inherit these guarantees automatically.",
